WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.302 --> 00:00:19.729
In half an hour Sherlock Holmes and I reached the house at 131 Pitt Street.

2
00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:27.968
As our cab pulled up, we found the sidewalk lined by a crowd of curious people, including several messenger boys. Holmes let out a low whistle.

3
00:00:27.969 --> 00:00:35.453
"It must be attempted murder at least, Watson," he said. "Nothing less will hold the attention of a London messenger boy and keep him from his work."

4
00:00:35.454 --> 00:00:39.708
Lestrade was looking out the front window as if eagerly anticipating our arrival.

5
00:00:39.709 --> 00:00:45.193
Before entering the house, Holmes inspected the top steps, which had been washed down with water.

6
00:00:45.194 --> 00:00:52.018
"There is no shortage of footprints," muttered Holmes. "Ah, Lestrade!" he said as the inspector opened the door for us.

7
00:00:52.019 --> 00:00:56.136
"It's the Napoleon bust business again, Mr. Holmes," said Lestrade.

8
00:00:56.137 --> 00:00:58.455
"What has happened?" asked Holmes.

9
00:00:58.455 --> 00:01:02.676
"A murder!" replied the inspector. "Come this way."

10
00:01:02.677 --> 00:01:08.348
Lestrade led us into a sitting room where a disheveled elderly man was pacing the floor in his flannel bathrobe.

11
00:01:08.349 --> 00:01:13.673
The inspector introduced him as Mr. Horace Harker, a journalist and the owner of the house.

12
00:01:13.674 --> 00:01:18.173
"Mr. Harker, will you tell these gentlemen exactly what has happened?" asked Lestrade.

13
00:01:18.174 --> 00:01:21.824
The man in the bathrobe looked at us with a melancholy face.

14
00:01:21.825 --> 00:01:29.079
"It's an extraordinary thing," said Mr. Harker. "All my life I've been writing other people's stories for the newspapers.

15
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:36.334
But now that a real piece of news has landed on my own doorstep, I am so confused and upset that I'm unable to write a word."

16
00:01:36.335 --> 00:01:40.739
"I can't help you if you don't tell us your story," said Holmes as we sat down.

17
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:49.147
"Well, I'll try," said Mr. Harker. "It all seems to involve that bust of Napoleon that I bought for this room about four months ago.

18
00:01:49.148 --> 00:01:52.560
I purchased it from the Harding Brothers store in the neighborhood."

19
00:01:52.561 --> 00:01:55.984
He told us that he was often up late working on his stories.

20
00:01:55.985 --> 00:02:01.602
Around three o'clock that morning he was writing in his upstairs den when he thought he heard noises downstairs.

21
00:02:01.603 --> 00:02:04.126
He listened carefully but heard nothing more.

22
00:02:04.126 --> 00:02:09.292
"Suddenly there came a horrible yell, the most dreadful sound that I've ever heard, Mr. Holmes.

23
00:02:09.293 --> 00:02:15.299
I sat frozen with horror for a minute. Then I seized the poker from the fireplace and went downstairs."

24
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.529
Mr. Harker entered the room in which we were now sitting and found the bust gone and the front window open.

25
00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:26.206
"I stepped out the front door and nearly fell over a dead man lying on the steps!" said Mr. Harker.

26
00:02:26.207 --> 00:02:35.706
"It was a most gruesome sight. I called for help and then I must have fainted because the next thing I remember, a policeman was standing over me in the front hall."

27
00:02:35.707 --> 00:02:38.611
"Who was the murdered man?" asked Holmes.

28
00:02:38.612 --> 00:02:46.599
"We don't know," said Lestrade, who went on to say that the man was tall, powerfully built, sunburned, and not more than thirty years old.

29
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:52.091
He was poorly dressed but didn't appear to be a laborer. A knife was found on the steps next to him.

30
00:02:52.092 --> 00:02:57.417
"We don't know whether the knife belonged to the murdered man or was the weapon that killed him," said Lestrade.

31
00:02:57.418 --> 00:03:04.683
"There was no identification on him and nothing in his pockets except an apple, some string, a cheap map of London, and this photograph."

32
00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:07.841
He showed us a picture of a man with thick, dark eyebrows.

33
00:03:07.842 --> 00:03:12.986
After a careful study of the photograph, Holmes asked, "And what became of the bust?"

34
00:03:12.987 --> 00:03:19.897
"We had news of it just before you arrived," said Lestrade. "It was discovered in the front yard of an empty house nearby.

35
00:03:19.898 --> 00:03:25.493
It was broken like the others. I'm going over there now to see it. Will you come with me?"

36
00:03:25.494 --> 00:03:29.154
"Certainly, but first I must take a look around," said Holmes.

37
00:03:29.155 --> 00:03:34.646
After he had thoroughly studied the room, the window, and the front door, we went in search of the broken statue.

38
00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:38.139
We found the pieces scattered over the lawn of the empty house.

39
00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:45.637
Holmes picked up several pieces and carefully examined them. From the intent look on his face, I was convinced that he had found a clue.

40
00:03:45.638 --> 00:03:48.708
"Well, Holmes, what do you think?" asked Lestrade.

41
00:03:48.709 --> 00:03:53.383
"It is revealing that the thief did not break the bust in the house or immediately outside it."

42
00:03:53.384 --> 00:03:59.123
"He was probably rattled by meeting that other fellow," said Lestrade. "The dead man."

43
00:03:59.124 --> 00:04:04.298
"Likely so, but what do you notice that's different about this house?" asked Holmes.

44
00:04:04.299 --> 00:04:09.116
"It's an empty house, so he knew he wouldn't be disturbed when he broke the bust," said Lestrade.

45
00:04:09.117 --> 00:04:12.862
"But we passed another empty house on our way to this one," I said.

46
00:04:12.863 --> 00:04:18.260
"Why didn't he break it there? Every extra step increased the chance that he'd meet someone on the street."

47
00:04:18.261 --> 00:04:22.848
Holmes pointed to the nearby streetlamp. "He could see what he was doing here."

48
00:04:22.849 --> 00:04:26.015
"So what are we to do with that fact?" asked Lestrade.

49
00:04:26.016 --> 00:04:32.428
"Remember it because it may come in useful later," said Holmes. "What steps do you propose to take now, Lestrade?"

50
00:04:32.429 --> 00:04:36.105
"The most practical way of solving the case is to identify the dead man.

51
00:04:36.106 --> 00:04:42.508
Then we'll have a good start on learning why he was on Pitt Street last night and who killed him. Don't you agree?"

52
00:04:42.509 --> 00:04:47.580
"Yes, and yet it's not quite the way that I would approach the case," said Holmes.

53
00:04:47.581 --> 00:04:49.994
"What would you do?" asked Lestrade.

54
00:04:49.995 --> 00:04:55.240
"Oh, you mustn't let me influence you. I suggest that you go your way and I'll go mine.

55
00:04:55.241 --> 00:05:02.994
But if you're going back to Pitt Street, tell Mr. Harker that the man in his house last night was a dangerous homicidal lunatic with a fixation on Napoleon."

56
00:05:02.995 --> 00:05:07.406
Lestrade raised his eyebrows. "You don't seriously believe that?"

57
00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:15.400
Holmes smiled. "Well, perhaps I don't. But I'm sure that my revelation will interest Mr. Harker and the readers of his newspaper."

58
00:05:15.401 --> 00:05:22.049
Holmes asked Lestrade if he might keep the photograph and arranged for the Scotland Yard inspector to meet us at Baker Street at six o'clock.

59
00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:30.058
Holmes and I then went on to the Harding Brothers shop where Mr. Harker had purchased his statue. But Mr. Harding was out until the afternoon.

60
00:05:30.059 --> 00:05:35.305
From there we made our way back to Morse Hudson's shop, a trip of almost ten miles.

61
00:05:35.306 --> 00:05:38.630
Morse Hudson was a short, stout man with a red face.

62
00:05:38.631 --> 00:05:46.116
"Why do we pay taxes for police protection when any thug could come in and break one's goods?" he asked with more than a trace of agitation.

63
00:05:46.117 --> 00:05:54.688
He told us that he had bought all three busts—the one that had been on his counter and the two belonging to Dr. Barnicot—from a sculpture factory called Gelder and Company.

64
00:05:54.689 --> 00:05:57.950
Holmes showed him the photograph of the man with bushy eyebrows.

65
00:05:57.951 --> 00:06:01.326
"Why, that's Beppo!" exclaimed Mr. Hudson.

66
00:06:01.327 --> 00:06:07.367
"He was an Italian fellow who used to do odd jobs for me around the shop. He could carve a little and frame pictures."

67
00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.850
"Where is he now?" asked Holmes.

68
00:06:09.851 --> 00:06:18.600
"I have no idea," replied Mr. Hudson. "He quit two days before the bust was smashed here, and I haven't heard from him since."
